Mild Oral Candidiasis
Common Infections and Afflictions
White adherent mucosal plaques, Erythematous base upon scraping, Cottony mouth sensation

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-028
Reason for Visit: Severe mouth burning, thick white coating on the tongue, and inability to taste food.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting acute, severe burning pain in the mouth accompanied by a thick, abnormal white coating over the tongue and throat following a recent course of broad-spectrum antibiotics. The patient is currently restricting fluid and nutritional intake due to severe odynophagia when attempting to consume anything acidic or textured. Urgent home assessment is clinically mandated to definitively assess the oral mucosa, rule out the extension of the fungal infection into the esophagus (esophageal candidiasis), and rapidly institute targeted antifungal therapy to restore pain-free oral intake. Delaying this assessment poses an unacceptable risk of escalating clinical dehydration, strictly fulfilling the criteria for MBS urgent after-hours item numbers 585-600.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: My mouth feels like it's stuffed full of burning cotton, and I can't even drink water without wincing.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 3 days ago, shortly after completing a 7-day course of broad-spectrum antibiotics for a respiratory infection. The patient describes a rapidly progressive "cottony" feeling in the mouth and a complete loss of taste. Over the past 48 hours, adherent white, curd-like plaques have developed extensively across the buccal mucosa and tongue. The patient experiences a severe, localized burning pain that is acutely exacerbated by acidic, spicy, or physically rough foods. Additionally, painful cracking at the corners of the mouth (angular cheilitis) has begun to develop. Denies difficulty swallowing solids (dysphagia to the stomach), chest pain, or systemic fevers.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently restricting oral intake due to severe localized discomfort.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. Moderate risk of dehydration due to fluid restriction secondary to pain.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 122/78 mmHg | HR: 86 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 71 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused. Frequent swallowing and visible discomfort when opening the mouth fully.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. A positive KOH preparation of mucosal scrapings showing budding yeasts and pseudohyphae would assist in confirming the diagnosis in atypical cases, but the clinical presentation is classic.
Physical Exam: Intraoral examination reveals widespread, adherent white and curd-like plaques heavily coating the dorsal tongue, hard palate, and bilateral buccal mucosa. When gently scraped with a tongue depressor, the plaques are removable, revealing a distinct, bright erythematous and friable mucosal base underneath. Dentition is normal and intact. The posterior pharynx is erythematous but the airway is patent. Bilateral angular cheilitis is present at the oral commissures. Submandibular lymph nodes are palpable but non-tender. Lungs are clear.
Environmental Observations: A recently emptied box of Amoxicillin-Clavulanate is visible on the kitchen counter.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient recently completed a 7-day course of prescribed broad-spectrum antibiotics.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Mild Oral Candidiasis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Leukoplakia - Definitively ruled out as the white plaques in this presentation are easily scraped away with a tongue depressor, whereas leukoplakic lesions are strictly non-removable and fixed to the mucosa.
Aphthous Stomatitis - Ruled out by the diffuse, widespread nature of the curd-like plaques and the lack of discrete, punched-out oval ulcers with erythematous halos.
Esophageal Candidiasis - Unlikely as the primary acute complication at this moment given the definitive absence of deep retrosternal pain upon swallowing, though close monitoring is required.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Living alone with restricted fluid intake)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The acute presentation of severe oral burning and thick mucosal plaques following antibiotic therapy severely restricted the patient's oral intake, creating an immediate risk of dehydration. Urgent on-site clinical evaluation was essential to confirm the diagnosis of oral thrush, visually exclude impending airway compromise from severe swelling, and definitively rule out signs of deeper esophageal involvement. By directly prescribing and instructing the use of potent topical antifungal agents, the intense localized pain could be rapidly controlled, immediately restoring the patient's ability to hydrate safely at home. This necessary stabilization could not safely be delayed until standard operating hours, satisfying MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Nystatin oral suspension (100,000 units/mL), 4 mL to be swished thoroughly around the mouth for 2 minutes and then swallowed, strictly four times daily for 7-14 days. Advised Paracetamol 1000mg up to four times daily for immediate analgesia to facilitate fluid intake.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maximize the chemical contact time of the antifungal medication against the yeast, do not eat or drink anything for exactly 30 minutes after swishing and swallowing the Nystatin. Rinsing your mouth with water immediately after the dose physically washes the medication away before it can effectively destroy the fungal cells.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 5-7 days to ensure the plaques are clearing and oral intake has fully normalized.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department immediately if you develop severe pain in the center of your chest when you swallow, if you become entirely unable to drink fluids, or if you develop a high fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; recurrent or persistent fungal infections warrant screening for underlying immunosuppressive conditions (e.g., undiagnosed diabetes).
Clinical Course: The acute burning pain typically begins to subside within 48 hours of starting the antifungal wash. The white plaques will gradually clear over 7 to 10 days. The medication must be continued for 48 hours after all visible signs have disappeared to prevent immediate relapse.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if you become dizzy upon standing, or if you do not pass urine for more than 8 hours, indicating severe dehydration.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The recent antibiotics destroyed the healthy, protective bacteria in your mouth, allowing the naturally occurring yeast to multiply out of control and coat your throat. The prescribed liquid is a targeted antifungal that acts as a localized weed-killer, stripping the yeast off the lining of your mouth.
Actionable Steps: Use a remarkably soft-bristled toothbrush to gently clean your teeth and tongue twice daily; immediately throw this toothbrush away and purchase a new one once the infection has cleared to prevent reinfecting yourself. Avoid all highly acidic beverages like orange juice and strictly consume cool, bland liquids until the raw tissue heals.

